    {
      "id": "676",
      "type": "related_content",
      "attributes": {
        "id": 676,
        "relationship_type": "related",
        "body": "Both forms use a double negative but differ in nuance and usage. ～ない～はない means 'There is no (A) that is not (B).' For example, 書けない漢字はない ('There are no kanji he can't write.') emphasizes complete ability. In contrast, ないことには～ない means 'unless', or literally 'If (A) can't happen, then (B) can't.'. For example, 漢字が書けないことには試験は合格できない ('You can't pass the test unless you can write kanji.').",
        "first_relatable": {
          "id": 378,
          "slug": "ない-はない",
          "title": "～ない～はない",
          "meaning": "There is not ~ that is not/does not",
          "level": "JLPT4",
          "type_pascal": "GrammarPoint",
          "type_snake": "grammar_point"
        },
        "second_relatable": {
          "id": 347,
          "slug": "ないことには-ない",
          "title": "ないことには～ない",
          "meaning": "Unless you do something",
          "level": "JLPT2",
          "type_pascal": "GrammarPoint",
          "type_snake": "grammar_point"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "713",
      "type": "related_content",
      "attributes": {
        "id": 713,
        "relationship_type": "related",
        "body": "Both forms use a double negative to convey a positive meaning, but differ in nuance and usage. ～ない～はない means 'There is no (A) that isn't (B).', e.g. 書けない漢字はない ('There are no kanji he can't write.'), strongly affirming his ability. In contrast, ないことはない softens a statement, implying 'It's not that (A) isn't true,' e.g., 書けないことはないけど ('It's not that I can't write.', i.e. I can, but perhaps not easily or well.).",
        "first_relatable": {
          "id": 378,
          "slug": "ない-はない",
          "title": "～ない～はない",
          "meaning": "There is not ~ that is not/does not",
          "level": "JLPT4",
          "type_pascal": "GrammarPoint",
          "type_snake": "grammar_point"
        },
